'''volar''' (vō′lär), ''a.'' [< ''vola'' + ''-ar''<sup>3</sup>.] '''1.''' Of or pertaining to the palm, especially the ball of the thumb; thenar: as, the ''volar'' artery (the superficialis volæ).—'''2.''' Palmar; not dorsal, as a side or aspect of the hand: as, the ''volar'' surface of the fingers.
{{smaller block|In many Mammals the limbs themselves, owing to the rich supply of nerves on their ''volar'' and plantar surfaces, and to the power of movement possessed by their terminal joints, have similar functions.
{{right|''Gegenbaur'', Comp. Anat. (trans.), p. 524.}}}}

'''volary†''' (vol′a-ri), ''n.'' See ''volery''.

'''volata''' (vō-lä′tä), ''n.'' In ''music'', a run, roulade, or division.

'''volatile''' (vol′a-til), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ME. ''volatil'', n., < OF. (and F.) ''volatil'' = Sp. ''volátil'' = Pg. ''volatil'' = It. ''volatile'', < L. ''volatilis'', flying, winged (LL. neut. ''volatile'', a winged creature, a fowl), < ''volare'', fly: see ''volant''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1†.''' Flying, or able to fly; having the power of flight; volant; volitant.
{{smaller block|The caterpillar towards the end of summer waxeth ''volatile'', and turneth to a butterfly. {{right|''Bacon'', Nat. Hist., § 728.}}}}
'''2.''' Having the quality of taking flight or passing off by spontaneous evaporation; evaporating rapidly; becoming diffused more or less freely in the atmosphere.
{{smaller block|It is anything but agreeable to be haunted by a suspicion that one’s intellect is . . . exhaling, without your consciousness, like ether out of a phial; so that, at every glance, you find a smaller and less ''volatile'' residuum.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Scarlet Letter, Int., p. 43.}}}}

