¶
{{smaller block|There are no fixtures in nature. The universe is fluid and ''volatile.''
{{right|''Emerson'', Circles.}}}}
'''3.''' Lively; brisk; gay; full of spirit; airy; hence, fickle; apt to change: as, a ''volatile'' disposition.
{{smaller block|You are as giddy and as ''volatile'' as ever.
{{right|''Swift'', To Gay, May 4, 1732.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>What do you care about a handsome youth?
They are so ''volatile'', and tease their wives!</poem>
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, II. 24.}}}}
'''4.''' Transient; not permanent; not lasting.
{{smaller block|''Volatile'' and fugitive instances of repentance.
{{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Repentance, v. § 6.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Volatile alkali,''' ammonia.—'''Volatile flycatcher.''' Same as ''volatile thrush.''—'''Volatile liniment,''' liniment of ammonia.—'''Volatile oil,''' an odorous vegetable principle having a strong pungent smell and taste, easily distilled with boiling water. The volatile oils contain no true fats, but are largely hydrocarbons. Also called ''essential oil.''—'''Volatile salts.''' See ''salt''<sup>1</sup>.—'''Volatile thrush.''' See ''Seisura.'' ='''Syn. 3.''' Changeable, giddy, flighty, inconstant. See ''volatility.''}}

'''II.'''† ''n.'' '''1.''' A winged creature, as a bird or butterfly.
{{smaller block|Make we man to oure ymage and likenesse, and be he sovereyn to the fischis of the see, and to the ''volatils'' of hevene, and to unresonable bestis of erthe.
{{right|''MS. Bodl.'' 277. (''Halliwell.'')}}}}
